WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.848 --> 00:00:06.015
- Sumo wrestling is a game of seconds.

00:00:07.763 --> 00:00:10.680
However, there's months of training

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.845
that go within those seconds.

00:00:14.845 --> 00:00:16.604
Imagine it’s like an earthquake

00:00:16.604 --> 00:00:19.869
that's building and building and building,

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:24.263
and all of the sudden, there's
this huge explosion,

00:00:24.263 --> 00:00:25.596
all at one time.

00:00:26.972 --> 00:00:28.639
That's what sumo is.

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:36.608
I'm really just a normal guy.

00:00:36.608 --> 00:00:37.774
I'm a husband.

00:00:37.774 --> 00:00:39.727
I'm a father of three.

00:00:39.727 --> 00:00:42.000
I'm the director of technology
for the county office

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.083
of education.

00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:47.270
I'm also an America sumo wrestler.

00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:51.464
- Sumo has a long history in Japan.

00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:55.189
Some people trace it back
up to 1,500 or 2,000 years,

00:00:55.189 --> 00:00:57.131
but it's essentially, you know, wrestling,

00:00:57.131 --> 00:00:59.803
where you either want to push
your opponent out of the ring,

00:00:59.803 --> 00:01:03.154
or knock your opponent to the ground.

00:01:03.154 --> 00:01:05.171
People have an image that sumo wrestlers

00:01:05.171 --> 00:01:07.059
are basically just bumping bellies,

00:01:07.059 --> 00:01:08.334
and wobbling around,

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:09.744
and as you can see from the practice here,

00:01:09.744 --> 00:01:12.494
nothing's further from the truth.

00:01:15.388 --> 00:01:18.162
Roy's amazing, he basically
came out of nowhere.

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:21.206
He showed up to the 2014 US Sumo Open,

00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:23.289
and blew everyone's mind.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:27.190
He beat every single American opponent.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.345
With almost no training,

00:01:28.345 --> 00:01:31.428
he's already the number one American.

00:01:37.343 --> 00:01:39.891
- To be a competitive sumo wrestler,

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.808
you need to train hard.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.297
Whether I'm stretching,
whether I'm power-lifting,

00:01:46.297 --> 00:01:49.214
whether I'm working out with rocks,

00:01:50.769 --> 00:01:53.436
I'm always looking for the edge.

00:01:54.596 --> 00:01:56.182
The thing that really brought me to sumo

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:59.251
was the fact that there
was culture and sport,

00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:00.805
all kinda mixed in one.

00:02:00.805 --> 00:02:03.757
It seemed like a perfect fit.

00:02:03.757 --> 00:02:06.401
For example, when you stomp the ground,

00:02:06.401 --> 00:02:09.894
you're ridding the area of bad spirits,

00:02:09.894 --> 00:02:11.840
and when you make your first clap,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.007
you're waking up the gods.

00:02:14.886 --> 00:02:16.568
- You know, he is a gentle giant,

00:02:16.568 --> 00:02:17.983
and he doesn't want to hurt anybody,

00:02:17.983 --> 00:02:19.233
and sumo's great that way,

00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:20.823
because, you know, they're just trying

00:02:20.823 --> 00:02:21.737
to get each other out of the ring,

00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:22.986
they're not trying to knock each other out

00:02:22.986 --> 00:02:25.236
or really hurt one another.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.141
- I think sumo has that camaraderie,

00:02:36.141 --> 00:02:38.892
fraternity aspect to it.

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:41.031
You know, we're banging   
heads, we're going at it.

00:02:41.031 --> 00:02:42.397
At the end of it, there's still

00:02:42.397 --> 00:02:44.814
this higher level of respect.

00:02:46.098 --> 00:02:50.265
I guess as a pacifist, that
definitely appeals to me.

